      en: "The Dakar Cathedral, officially known as the Cathédrale du Souvenir Africain, is a striking religious landmark in the heart of the Senegalese capital. Constructed during the 1920s and 1930s, it was consecrated on February 2, 1936, by the Archbishop of Paris. The architectural design by Charles-Albert Wulffleff uniquely blends Byzantine elements with Sudano-Sahelian styles, giving the building an eclectic and monumental appearance. Built on the site of a former cemetery, the cathedral was originally intended to honor the African soldiers who fought for France. The interior is grand, featuring a massive central dome adorned with frescoes that depict biblical scenes alongside African saints. As the seat of the Archdiocese of Dakar, it serves as the center of Catholic life in Senegal and stands as a symbol of the country's peaceful interfaith coexistence. Its brilliant white facade and twin towers remain iconic features of the Dakar-Plateau skyline.",
